  {
   "id": "A8",
   "what": "We the People layer item: credits and description cite the Census Bureau's 1791 return.",
   "kind": "item-fields",
   "item": "43ae0255f8ed46d5973871603f5956dd",
   "fields": {
    "description": [
     "<p>House seats under the apportionment act of 14 April 1792 (105 seats, one for every 33,000 people counted under the three-fifths clause), with the seats each state would have had if enslaved people had been counted in full (<b>SeatsIfAll</b>) or not at all (<b>SeatsIfNone</b>).</p><p>The 1790 census figures are as tabulated in Wikipedia's article on the 1790 census, from the Census Bureau's <i>Heads of Families</i> volumes. Under the act's rule they reproduce every state's seats, which checks them.</p><p>Today's state outlines (Natural Earth, public domain), with Maine shown as part of Massachusetts and West Virginia as part of Virginia.</p><p><i>Version 1.0 (14 October 2026).</i></p>",
     "<p>House seats under the apportionment act of 14 April 1792 (105 seats, one for every 33,000 people counted under the three-fifths clause), with the seats each state would have had if enslaved people had been counted in full (<b>SeatsIfAll</b>) or not at all (<b>SeatsIfNone</b>).</p><p>The 1790 census figures are from the Census Bureau's <i>Return of the Whole Number of Persons within the Several Districts of the United States</i> (1791). Under the act's rule they reproduce every state's seats, which checks them.</p><p>Today's state outlines (Natural Earth, public domain), with Maine shown as part of Massachusetts and West Virginia as part of Virginia.</p><p><i>Version 1.1 (23 October 2026).</i></p>"
    ],
    "accessInformation": [
     "Seats: National Archives. Ratio: U.S. Census Bureau. 1790 census figures: Wikipedia (from Census Bureau volumes). Outlines: Natural Earth (public domain).",
     "Seats: National Archives. Ratio: U.S. Census Bureau. 1790 census figures: U.S. Census Bureau, Return of the Whole Number of Persons (1791). Outlines: Natural Earth (public domain)."
    ]
   }
  },
